<SiteLayout title="Plan Your Trip — Just Okinawa" description="Start planning your Okinawa journey with flexible itinerary ideas and practical links for transport, seasons and island travel."><div class="wrap"><header class="page-intro"><span class="eyebrow">YOUR NEXT CHAPTER STARTS HERE</span><h1>A little planning.<br/><em>A lot to look forward to.</em></h1><p>Choose an island first. Leave room for the unexpected. These are flexible starting points, not fixed schedules.</p></header><section class="content-section"><div class="guide-grid"><article class="guide-card" id="first-visit"><span class="eyebrow">3 DAYS / MAIN ISLAND</span><h2>A little of everything.</h2><ol><li><strong>Settle into Naha.</strong><p>Leave your arrival day flexible. Explore nearby streets and find somewhere local for dinner.</p></li><li><strong>Choose your coast.</strong><p>Plan a coastal outing around your accommodation and transport. Choose a few stops rather than covering the entire island.</p></li><li><strong>Make space for culture.</strong><p>Explore a heritage or craft experience, with enough time to get back for your departure.</p></li></ol><a class="text-link" href="/islands/main/">Explore the main island ↗</a></article><article class="guide-card" id="slow-islands"><span class="eyebrow">5 DAYS / YAEYAMA</span><h2>Less rushing. More living.</h2><ol><li><strong>Days 1–2: Begin on Ishigaki.</strong><p>Get your bearings and explore at an easy pace. Choose activities that fit your base and the weather.</p></li><li><strong>Day 3: Take a side trip.</strong><p>Consider Taketomi after checking ferry times. Respect residential lanes and private homes.</p></li><li><strong>Days 4–5: Keep some room.</strong><p>Choose another local experience, or simply stay longer somewhere you love. Keep a buffer before your flight.</p></li></ol><a class="text-link" href="/islands/yaeyama/">Explore Yaeyama ↗</a></article></div><div class="essentials" id="essentials"><span class="eyebrow">THE PRACTICAL LITTLE THINGS</span><h2>Before you go.</h2><details open><summary>Which island should I choose?</summary><p>Think about how you want to spend your days: a mix of city and coast, a beach-focused stay, or time on smaller islands. Start with <a href="/#islands">our island explorer</a>, then check transport before combining destinations.</p></details><details><summary>How do I get there and get around?</summary><p>Choose the gateway that suits your destination. Check current flight, ferry and local transport information through the <a href="https://visitokinawajapan.com/plan-your-trip/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official Okinawa travel planner ↗</a>. Allow for weather-related changes to island travel.</p></details><details><summary>When should I visit?</summary><p>The kind of trip you want matters as much as the season. Check the <a href="https://visitokinawajapan.com/plan-your-trip/when-to-visit/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official seasonal guide ↗</a> and current weather. Keep outdoor plans flexible.</p></details><details><summary>How can I be a thoughtful visitor?</summary><p>Follow local signs and swimming guidance, keep off coral, take your litter with you, and ask before photographing people. Village lanes are also people’s homes: respect private property and keep noise down.</p></details></div></section></div></SiteLayout>
